[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m <documents>Doc 1(Title: "High roller") High roller A high roller, also referred to as a whale, is a gambler who consistently wagers large amounts of money. High rollers often receive lavish ""comps"" from casinos to lure them onto the gambling floors, such as free private jet transfers, limousine use and use of the casinos' best suites. Casinos may also extend credit to a player to continue betting, offer rebates on betting turnover or losses, and salaries of employees may also contain incentive arrangements to bring in high rollers. The definition of a high roller varies. At Crown Casino in Australia, for example, it involves bringing
[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "High Rollers Casino") High Rollers Casino High Rollers Casino is a 2004 casino video game co-developed by Cinemaware and Spanish studio Virtual Toys for the PlayStation 2 and Xbox gaming consoles. The game allows players to partake in various games typically found within a casino, ranging from card to slot machines. Upon beginning the game, the player is tasked with the creation of a character. After one is made, they are placed within the casino itself, a 3D room, and given full control of their avatar. To begin playing, the player must choose a game they would like to play from a selection of games, primarily based on the casino to which they have been transported. After choosing a game, a digital selection feature is activated, similar to the skill shot in the games of the Extreme Sports franchise. Upon selection, the player resumes control of their avatar and starts playing, akin to a live player's experience. High Rollers Casino represents a direct adaptation of the Phoenix Club, a highly profitable casino in Las Vegas, created by Sir Richard Branson. While the setting may appear to be a generic casino, players have been taken to other casinos within the game. In-game features such as television broadcasts of games, advertisements for special offers, advertisements for resort amenities, and advertisements for credit promotions are present. Upon finishing a game, the player is returned to the casino itself and allowed to either choose a new game or leave the casino altogether. Virtual Toys stated they aimed to ensure the games offered within the casino were "crack [sic] emulators" of what the player could expect in a Las Vegas casino. At the time of release, the game included slot machines, blackjack, three-card poker, and Keno variants. The game also included a split-screen second player option. Virtual Toys released a PlayStation 3 version in 2005. The game was created for the gaming console market, with some features, such as the ability to adjust the table size on the slot machines, being removed. Cinemaware released an Xbox version in 2006. The game has been ported to the Steam digital platform and has received a 2008 compilation of previous titles and new content for PlayStation Network, Microsoft Windows and iOS, titled Virtual Toys: High Rollers Casino Collection. Virtual Toys released a Nintendo Wii version in 2010. Virtual Toys announced a mobile game in 2013. </documents>
