[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "Siege of Bharatpur") by the Governor-General of India, Lord Amherst. Before his death Baldeo Singh had entrusted his son to the protection of distinguished General Sir David Ochterlony who, acting on his own initiative as Civil Commissioner, advanced on Bharatpore with an army from the British garrison at Delhi. When Amherst heard of the move, he sent per letter, giving the directions necessary for the surprise which, not only accomplished the entrance into the gate of the castle but also the actual capture of it. Ochterlony was Lieut. Governor, Commander-in-Chief of India in Bengal, and Major General, Commander-in-Chief of the Bengal army. Baldeo Singh was the son of the Raja of Bharatpore. Alexander Cheyne was Governor of the Company's territories of Delhi and Upper India, who was appointed to command the siege force until 11 February 1805.
