[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: Moyross) Moyross Moyross (Irish: ""Maigh Ruis"") is a council estate in Limerick, Ireland. It was developed in the 1970s and 1980s, and, up to 2008, the estate was unusual in that it spanned two electoral areas, with 728 houses part of the Ballynanty DED of Limerick City Council, and 432 houses part of the Ardmore DED. The housing estate is home to the local authority bus depot, DED headquarters and an AA repair station. Moyross has a large mixed-race population and has been the setting for crime dramas, most notably the BBC and Sky TV series "Tiger Bay". Moyross is a small urban village adjacent to the docks. The housing estate has no public transport links, has no communal amenities, no shops and is regularly subject to violent crime.
[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 3(Title: Community) Community Community is an urban settlement on the Foyle River in the County Donegal Borough Council area in Northern Ireland. Community is an area of mixed housing built around the railway lines that converge in the village. The Foyle Cross Bridge runs under the railway lines. It is just off the A2 from Strands and farther north it borders the Bluestone Housing Estate and Creggan housing estate.
[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 4(Title: Beaumaris) Beaumaris Beaumaris (from the old Irish Beal an Mhairí "the mouth of the mare" meaning "the gap") is a community in the northwest of Derry City and Strabane District Council. It is located within a mile of the border with the Republic of Ireland. The community is a public housing estate and a large housing estate comprising numerous single family houses and apartments. Beaumaris is currently one of the fastest growing communities in the Western Region. Beaumaris is the site of the land-based component of the Premier Hotel Derry.
