[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m <documents>Doc 1(Title: "Zapruder film") Zapruder film The Zapruder film is a silent 8mm color motion picture sequence shot by private citizen Abraham Zapruder with a Bell & Howell home-movie camera, as U.S. President John F. Kennedy's motorcade passed through Dealey Plaza in Dallas, Texas on November 22, 1963. Unexpectedly, it ended up capturing the President's assassination. Even though it is not the only film of the shooting, the Zapruder film has been described as being the most complete one, giving a relatively clear view from a somewhat elevated position on the side from which the president's fatal head wound is visible. It was an
[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "Zapruder film") important part of the Warren Commission hearings and all subsequent investigations of the assassination, and it is one of the most studied pieces of film in history. Of greatest notoriety is the film's capture of the fatal shot to President Kennedy's head when his presidential limousine was almost exactly in front of, and slightly below, Zapruder's position. Abraham Zapruder stood on a concrete pedestal along Elm Street in Dealey Plaza holding a high-end movie camera to obtain this. He threatened to deny access to the film after the assassination until the FBI obliged him. He retained an interest in the film for 42 years, until he died in 1991. The film was an important part of the Warren Commission investigation of President Kennedy's assassination of November 22, 1963. Later, the Zapruder film was purchased for $1.5 million by a private organization and is permanently preserved at the John F. Kennedy Center for Technology & Culture at Fair Park in Dallas, where it has been displayed in the John F. Kennedy Legacy Center for many years. It is now owned by the National Park Service. It is the largest theater screen in the world and may only be viewed once a day. It is not generally public display material because of the constitutionally protected status of the film. Information below is from the Zapruder Family Foundation's website.</documents>
