[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "Flow measurement") open channels. Measuring flow in waterways is an important open-channel flow application; such installations are known as stream gauges. The level of the water is measured at a designated point behind weir or in flume using various secondary devices (bubblers, ultrasonic, float, and differential pressure are common methods). This depth is converted to a flow rate according to a theoretical formula of the form formula_8 where formula_1 is the flow rate, formula_10 is a constant, formula_11 is the water level, and formula_12 is a constant. Flow is commonly measured by a flume, a sharp-faced truncated prism often designed to require a reference datum (formula_13). The reference datum is the reference datum flow (RDF), which is the depth of the water at which the flow rate equals 1.0 The primary device that measures the water depth is a range rod, which may be robotic or manned. A range rod's measurement may be supplemented with photographic methods for monitoring flow depth in remote areas. A current meter is an instrument for measuring the speed of a stream. It may be launched from a boat, or suspended from a buoy or float, at regular intervals along the mean depth. A current meter may also be designated as a measuring device to measure velocity.  A current meter calculates the average velocity by noting the distance traveled by the float or sphere and the time taken. An acoustical current meter uses ultrasonic sound waves to measure velocity and is generally preferred over traditional current meter devices because they do not require contact with the water. The alternate techniques include the use of an acoustic Doppler current profiler (ADCP), which measures velocity and allows for measurement of flow velocity with high accuracy. This instrument transmits sound waves perpendicular to the flow and detects their deflection to calculate velocity profiles. It can be used to measure the volume flux of a flow, determining discharge</documents>
