[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "Russian Revolution") extensive democratic reforms in Russia (the replacement of the monarchy by a republic, guaranteed civil rights, a democratic police and army, abolition of religious and ethnic discrimination, preparation of elections to a constituent assembly, and so on). They met in the same building as the emerging Provisional Government not to compete with the Duma Committee for state power, but to best exert pressure on the new government, to act, in other words, as a popular democratic lobby. The relationship between these two major powers was complex from the beginning and tended to shift direction with the changing fortunes of the revolution. The Provisional Government was a coalition of the Duma majority that included reformist liberals and centrists, conservatives, and agrarians. The coalition was based on what Marxists and anarchists called the February "Workers' Programme" (the inclusion of support for a workers' movement and a pro-Soviet alliance), the party programme of
[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 3(Title: "Russian Revolution") The February Revolution of 1917 overthrew the Romanovs and established the Provisional Government. Its members, including the Kadets, appointed to the Duma by the imperial government as the backward stage of the revolutionary process was now past. At the February Revolution, the masses had the definitive decision for the replacement of the Tsar with a provisional government. What remained to be settled was the relationship between the government and the Revolutionary Committees and those directing mass demonstrations. Democratic and social demands from the masses were immediate and overwhelming. The Bolsheviks advocated a permanent revolutionary movement that would continue the revolution beyond the limited goals of the February Revolution. They had the dual purpose of replacing the Provisional Government with the Soviets of Workers' and Soldiers' Deputies, and of overthrowing the government in power and replacing it with the Soviets. At the same time, Lenin had 
[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 4(Title: "Russian Revolution") As of the November Revolution of 1917, whereas the Provisional Government was a coalition of liberals, centrists, conservatives, and agrarians, the Bolsheviks were supported by working-class elements, left-wing socialists, non-Russian minorities, and the peasantry. The Working-Classes wanted the land seized from the landlords and distributed to the peasants. The Peasants wanted the land seized from the landlords and distributed to the peasants. The Soviets of Workers' and Soldiers' Deputies. The Bolsheviks demanded the overthrow of the Provisional Government and the establishment of Soviet power.</documents>
