[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "Maxwell Land Grant") the United States Supreme Court: Maxwell Land Grant The Maxwell Land Grant, also known as the Beaubien-Miranda Land Grant, was a Mexican land grant in Colfax County, New Mexico and part of adjoining Las Animas County, Colorado. This 1841 land grant was one of the largest contiguous private landholdings in the history of the United States. The New Mexico towns of Cimarron, Colfax, Dawson, Elizabethtown, French, Lynn, Maxwell, Miami, Raton, Rayado, Springer, Ute Park and Vermejo Park, came to be located within the grant, as well as numerous other towns that are now ghost towns. The lands covered in the Maxwell Land Grant were originally occupied by the Indigenous Pueblo peoples, including the Taos, Picuris, Taos, Tesuque, and San Domingo. The Spanish gave the land to the Pueblo peoples as part of the Spanish encomienda system, which was a form of pre-Conquest servitude in which the Pueblo Indians were obligated to provide labor and tribute to the Spanish colonists. During the New Mexico territorial period, the United States government passed laws allowing for the settlement of the United States citizens and granting them title to land in New Mexico. The Maxwell Land Grant was not a title of the United States, but a grant of land issued by Mexico to the Beaubien-Miranda family.</documents>
