[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m <documents>Doc 1(Title: "Eyewitness testimony") and states within the US are now attempting to make changes in how eyewitness testimony is presented in court. Eyewitness testimony is a specialized focus within cognitive psychology. Psychologists have probed the reliability of eyewitness testimony since the beginning of the 20th century. One prominent pioneer was Hugo Münsterberg, whose controversial book ""On the Witness Stand"" (1908) demonstrated the fallibility of eyewitness accounts, but met with fierce criticism, particularly in legal circles. His ideas did, however, gain popularity with the public. Decades later, DNA testing would clear individuals convicted on the basis of errant eyewitness testimony. Studies by Scheck, Neufel,
[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: Optography) 1877, when Berlin police photographed the eyes of murder victim Frau von Sabatzky, on the chance that the image would assist in solving the crime. In 1888, London police officer Walter Dew—later known for catching the murderer Dr Crippen—recalled optography being attempted on Mary Jane Kelly in what he called a ""forlorn hope"" of catching her suspected killer, Jack the Ripper. Ripperologist James Stewart-Grimshaw writes of William Francis Blake who “went on the look-out for the parents” of a murder victim, and “indeed was the first person in the world ever to do so”. According to Stewart-Grimshaw, Blake failed to assist the police in catching Jack the Ripper, and escaped retribution and punishment. Mary Ellen Wesson wrote the first guide to the use of eye witness testimonies in the courtroom in 1898. In the United States, one of the first criminal cases involving the testimony of an uncontacted eye witness was that of Mary Austin and Edward Stead in 1889. In the United States, early historical precedent for eye witness testimonies was recognized in 1896, when the Supreme Court of Missouri first ruled that eyewitness testimony was admissible, in McAnnally vs. Oregon. In addition, a few short decades after the Second World War, work was performed in the UK by Canadian researcher Vic Caleb and others into the reliability of eye witness identification. Their work was led by the Developmental Laboratory of the Keele
[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 3(Title: "Memory and Eyewitness Testimony") research has continued on eye witness identification, providing more insights into the conditions under which eyewitness memory may be most reliable. The research has continued to examine the impact of various factors on eye witness accuracy, including stress during witnessing events, bias in reporting, misinformation, and changes in memory over time. Memory for things people have seen, but not been directly addressed by, is often poor. Eyewitness identification involves complex psychological processes and is susceptible to a range of misleading suggestions. Responding to legal needs, polygraphy developed as a scientific tool to aid judges in assessing witness reliability. Methods including lineups and simultaneous identification lineups, blind lineup presentation, and sequential display lineup methods have been developed. Retrospective analysis of video evidence has been a topic of research. A growing body of knowledge on eye witness accuracy has been developed in the 1980s. Recent research on eye witness accuracy has explored the impact of factors such as stress during witnessing events, bias in reporting, and misinformation on accuracy, and the effectiveness of various line-up methods in aiding judges. In recent years, some legal standards have been revisited and relaxed in light of research findings regarding the reliability of eye witness testimony. On the other hand, the "pictorial lineup" has been criticized for potentially reducing the rate of identification by adding distractors, and a blind display lineup method has been shown to be more effective than the traditional lineup in certain situations. Findings from memory studies have been systematically employed to examine the validity of eye witness testimony in court cases. As a result, a judge may exclude eye witness testimony as unreliable if it is subjected to certain legal criteria. Despite the credibility crisis regarding eyewitness testimony, people still believe that eye witnesses are good in identifying and identifying with a high degree of accuracy, an effect known as the hindsight bias.</documents>
