[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "French drop") French drop The French drop, also known as """"Le Tourniquet"""", is a sleight of hand method used by magicians to vanish a small object such as a coin. It is one of the oldest methods of vanishing, however it is still effective when properly executed. Although the method is known as a vanish, it can also be used as a switch or transformation, giving rise to numerous possibilities. The object is held between the thumb and first two finger tips. When using a coin, the magician covers the coin with a finger to keep it from glancing off the other hand. During the execution, the magician must visualize the coin going "down" to disappear. The coin is commonly dropped back into the hands of the magician's assistant. In the audience's eyes, the coin appears to be transferred to the spectator's hand, then lost in a pocket or behind the back.
[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 3(Title: "French drain") French drain A French drain is a drainage system used to collect water beneath and around structures. French drains are typically installed to reduce water infiltration, prevent erosion, and improve soil health. A French drain is a type of drainage structure, consisting of a perforated pipe (or plastic trench), filter fabric, and backfill material. The goal of a French drain is to convey water away from a specific area, typically a building or foundation. The backfill material is usually gravel or aggregate, and the pipe or trench is buried underground to allow for drainage. A French drain may be designed to exit at the surface, where water can be collected and directed away from the structure. Alternatively, it can exit at a slope, into a ditch, or into a watercourse, depending on the specific application and local conditions. A French drain is often constructed of permeable materials like gravel, and is intended to allow water to flow through it rather than being retained. A French drain is a drainage system, typically used underground to manage water runoff beneath and around buildings. A French drain typically has a perforated pipe buried underground, with filter fabric and gravel or aggregate around it. The goal is to convey water away from a building or foundation. Backfill material can be gravel or aggregate, and water is intended to flow through it rather than be retained. In the UK, local authorities may have specific requirements for the outfall of a French drain. </documents>
