[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m <documents>Doc 1(Title: "Great Famine (Ireland)") was responsible for a significant increase in emigration from Ireland, of anywhere from 45% to nearly 85% depending on the year and the county, it was not the sole cause. The beginning of mass emigration from Ireland can be traced to the mid-18th century, when some 250,000 people left Ireland over a period of 50 years to settle in the New World. Irish economist Cormac Ó Gráda estimates that between 1 million and 1.5 million people emigrated during the 30 years between 1815 (when Napoleon was defeated in Waterloo) and 1845 (when the Great Famine began). However, during the worst
[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "Irish diaspora") of the mass emigration from Ireland being a ""flight from famine"" is not entirely correct: firstly, the Irish had been coming to build canals in Great Britain since the 18th century, and once conditions were better emigration did not slow down. After the famine was over the four following years produced more emigrants than during the four years of the blight. Second, the terms "great famine" and "famine" are misused: the Famine had nothing to do with a famine in time and nothing to do with a famine in severity: it killed fewer people than the potato blight of the 1840s killed on the Continent of Europe. The major Irish epidemics (peverley, malaria, etc.) had struck the country before this. Third, Thomas M Kennelly reports that little emigration occurred from Ireland during the most trying months (July 1846 - November 1847) of the Blight. Fourth, population estimates for the period circa 1845 were inflated (1,000,000 compared to an estimated 1,300,000 in 1843).
[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 3(Title: "Ineffectual interventions to stop emigration from Ireland") Famine relief was inadequate due to the hostility of the authorities to famine relief and of Parliament to the size and cost of the undertaking; the best aid against the emigration was English relief, not Irish relief; the Government was misinformed and acted on advice from Irish landlords not accurate; Ireland had many shortages of labor and needed the emigration. Dr. Peddie points out that in 1846 91% of Irishmen and 99% of Irish women outside the famine counties were literate whereas 80% in 1843 already in the famine counties was similar literate. The reason for such a sharp drop can best be explained by the policies of the “Landlords”; renting the few quarter acres of landlord land and no rents at all was absurd. An analysis of land records has revealed that between 1831 and 1841 the emigration rate increased by 30% and by 1846 it reached 250,000 in a year.</documents>
