[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "Maxwell Land Grant") the United States Supreme Court: Maxwell Land Grant The Maxwell Land Grant, also known as the Beaubien-Miranda Land Grant, was a Mexican land grant in Colfax County, New Mexico and part of adjoining Las Animas County, Colorado. This 1841 land grant was one of the largest contiguous private landholdings in the history of the United States. The New Mexico towns of Cimarron, Colfax, Dawson, Elizabethtown, French, Lynn, Maxwell, Miami, Raton, Rayado, Springer, Ute Park and Vermejo Park, came to be located within the grant, as well as numerous other towns that are now ghost towns. The lands covered in the Maxwell Land Grant were originally owned by the Taos, Rio Arriba, and Mora Pueblos of New Mexico and the eastern slope Pueblo of Taos. When New Mexico became a U.S. territory in 1846, the Grant was declared an 'Indian Country' and was the center of a controversy. Question: What was the purpose of the Maxwell Land Grant?</documents>
