[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: Thermocline) layer. The warmer, less dense air then rises, destroying the stability that characterizes the nightly inversion. This phenomenon was first applied to the field of noise pollution study in the 1960s, contributing to the design of urban highways and noise barriers. Thermocline A thermocline (also known as the thermal layer or the metalimnion in lakes) is a thin but distinct layer in a large body of fluid (e.g. water, as in an ocean or lake; or air, e.g. an atmosphere) in which there is a nearly horizontal stratification of temperature. The term comes from the Greek τερμός (termós) meaning “hot” and Κλίμακας (klíma kakas) meaning “scale” or “ramp”. In oceanography, the temperature of the water at the surface is typically several degrees Celsius higher than that of the deeper water. This is referred to as the thermocline. In lakes, the temperature of the water at the surface is generally about 4 °C (39 °F) at sea level, and the temperature of the warmer surface water is typically 1-2 °C higher than that of the deeper water. In fresh water lakes, the denser, colder water is below the thermocline, while in salt water lakes, the denser, warmer water is below the thermocline. The layer of water below the thermocline in a fresh water lake is denser and colder than the water above it and can become extremely cold, forming an area with low oxygen levels. If the temperature at the surface of the lake reaches the same temperature as the water below, thermal inversion occurs. Thermoclines occur in both fresh and saltwater bodies, and can be vertical or horizontal depending on the conditions. The boundary between the surface and the thermocline in a lake is known as the epilimnion, and the boundary between the thermocline and the deeper water is known as the hypolimnion. Below the thermocline in fresh water lakes, the water is often referred to as the hypolimnion, which is the coldest and most heavily stratified layer of the lake. In lakes, the water is denser in the hypolimnion compared to the surface waters and warmer water is above it. In summary, in a lake, the term "thermocline" refers to the distinct layer of nearly horizontal stratification of temperature, while the term "hypolimnion" refers to the layer of water below the thermocline that is denser and colder. </documents>
