[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m <documents>Doc 1(Title: "Souffle (heart sound)") is synchronous with the pulse of the foetus, usually heard during the second trimester of pregnancy (13–28 weeks). It is heard because of the rush of blood through the umbilical arteries of the foetus, and is therefore synchronous with the foetal pulse. The funic souffle is not heard consistently. On auscultation, funic souffle may often be confused with uterine souffle, which is a soft, blowing sound synchronous with the maternal pulse. Therefore, the maternal pulse must be palpated simultaneously in order to differentiate uterine souffle from funic souffle. A mammary souffle is a maternal cardiac murmur heard over the breasts.
[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "Heart sounds") Heart sounds Heart sounds are the noises generated by the beating heart and the resultant flow of blood through it. Specifically, the sounds reflect the turbulence created when the heart valves snap shut. In cardiac auscultation, an examiner may use a stethoscope to listen for these unique and distinct sounds that provide important auditory data regarding the condition of the heart. In healthy adults, there are two normal heart sounds, the first heart sound (S1) and the second heart sound (S2). The first heart sound is synchronous with the ventricular systole. The second heart sound is synchronous with the aortic and pulmonic valve closure. The aortic valve closure is accompanied by the pressure wave of aorta pulsation. The second heart sound is heard near the second intercostal space on the left sternal border and is a loud, double-pitched "lub" sound. The pulmonic valve closure may be faint or absent in some individuals, and may be heard within one beat of the second heart sound. The pulmonary valve closure is the final significant sound of the heartbeat, and is heard near the third intercostal space on the left sternal border and is a softer "lou" sound. The tricuspid valve closure sound may be faint. The mitral valve closure is typically the first heart sound (S1) of the heartbeat, and is heard near the fifth intercostal space on the left sternal border and is a soft, fluttering "dub" sound. </documents>
