[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "Legal status of tattooing in the United States") Legal status of tattooing in the United States In the United States, there is no federal law regulating the practice of tattooing. However, all 50 states and the District of Columbia have statutory laws requiring a person receiving a tattoo be 18 years or older. This is partially based on the legal principle that a minor cannot enter into a legal contract or otherwise render informed consent for a procedure. Most states permit a person under the age of 18 to receive a tattoo with consent from a parent or guardian. Documentation of the parent's consent may vary by state. In New Hampshire, for instance, a parental consent form is required, but it does not need to be notarized. In Georgia, for example, a parent or legal guardian is required to sign a consent form prior to a minor receiving a tattoo, and the minor must also sign an informed consent form. In New Hampshire, a parent's signature alone may be sufficient for a child to receive a tattoo, but only with parental consent. No federal law regulates the practice of tattooing. An existing state statute in New Hampshire requires that no person under the age of 18 receive a tattoo without consent from a parent or guardian, and the minor must sign an informed consent form as well. There is no minimum age for a first tattoo in any state. Any prior tattoo does not need parental consent. Each state has its own specific laws requiring a signed consent form from the parent or guardian prior to any tattoo procedure on a minor. Despite being a constitutional amendment, the Missouri “Religious Freedom, Protection, and Civil Rights Act” does not apply to the regulation of tattooing. The Washington state legislature is currently considering a bill to regulate tattooing in Washington. </doc2> </documents>
