[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "Planned maintenance") Planned maintenance Planned preventive maintenance (PPM), more commonly referred to as simply planned maintenance (PM) or scheduled maintenance, is any variety of scheduled maintenance to an object or item of equipment. Specifically, planned maintenance is a scheduled service visit carried out by a competent and suitable agent, to ensure that an item of equipment is operating correctly and to therefore avoid any unscheduled breakdown and downtime. Along with condition-based maintenance, planned maintenance comprises preventive maintenance, in which the maintenance event is preplanned, and all future maintenance is preprogrammed. Planned maintenance is created for Preventive maintenance, also known as Planned Preventive Maintenance (PPM), includes scheduled service visits and corrective maintenance. Planned maintenance can include scheduled service visits, which are typically scheduled based on factors such as time intervals between checks, hours of operation, or the amount of usage. Timed interval schedules are divided into the following three categories: routine, repetitive, and periodic, with routine scheduled maintenance being the most frequent, typically monthly to annual, repetitive maintenance being less frequent, typically quarterly to semi-annually, and periodic maintenance being the least frequent, typically every few years or after reaching a specific equipment age or condition. Fixed-schedule maintenance involves scheduled maintenance planned at specific intervals without requiring condition-based analysis. Life cycle maintenance is an event-based approach to maintenance planning, where maintenance is scheduled when equipment reaches a certain stage in its usage, rather than based on time or usage. Planned maintenance can be categorized as either reactive or preventive, depending on whether it is focused on fixing issues before they occur or addressing issues that have already occurred. Common maintenance practices include using tags or maintenance plans, and Electronic maintenance management system (EMMS) can automate the process. Scheduling should consider factors such as machine downtime, equipment failure rates, production schedule, work environment, age of the equipment, regulatory requirements, and safety issues. </documents>
