[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: Bed) x 190 cm), the sizes for other bed types tend to vary. Mainland European sizes differ, not merely because of the use of the metric system. In the mid-1950s, the United States bedding industry introduced a new size: the king size. A king-sized bed differs from the other sizes in implementation, as it is not common to have a king-sized box spring; rather, two smaller box-springs are used under a king-sized mattress. It is a common misconception that in a king-sized bed, the two box-springs are both as large as the mattress on top; however, one is typically the size of a standard twin mattress, and the other is larger (often a full or queen size), designed to fit the bed. In the 1960s, additional bedding sizes were introduced: the super king size (also known as the king-size European or California king), and the super super king size (also known as the California queen). This led to further confusion with the metric system, as the king and super king sizes are approximately equivalent to the 150 cm and 180 cm formats, respectively, while the super super king size is approximately equivalent to the 200 cm format. </documents>
