[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m <documents>Doc 1(Title: "Delaware State University") Delaware State University Delaware State University (DSU or Del State), is a historically black, public university in Dover, Delaware. DSU also has two satellite campuses, one in Wilmington and one in Georgetown. The university encompasses four colleges and a diverse population of undergraduate and advanced-degree students. The Delaware College for Colored Students was established on May 15, 1891, by the Delaware General Assembly. The name was changed to the State College for Colored Students by state legislative action in 1893 to eliminate confusion with Delaware College, which was attended by whites in Newark, Del. It first awarded degrees in 1898.
[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "University of Delaware") University of Delaware The University of Delaware (colloquially UD, UDel, or U of D) is a public research university located in Newark, Delaware. University of Delaware is the largest university in Delaware. UD currently offers more than 135 undergraduate degrees. At the graduate level, it offers 67 doctoral, 142 master’s degree programs, 14 dual degrees, 15 interdisciplinary programs, 12 on-line programs, and 3 dual undergraduate certificates. The library system of the university is the third largest in Delaware. The University's total enrollment exceeds 28,000 students as of Fall 2023. It has been ranked among the top public schools in the nation by U.S. News & World Report in five consecutive years ending in 2021.
