[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "The Little Prince") Despite a raucous marriage, Saint-Exupéry kept Consuelo close to his heart and portrayed her as the prince's rose, whom he tenderly protects with a wind screen and places under a glass dome on his tiny planet. Saint-Exupéry's infidelity and the doubts of his marriage are symbolized by the vast field of roses the prince encounters during his visit to Earth. This interpretation was described by biographer Paul Webster who stated she was ""the little prince's Earth Rose"". The only human on the prince's planet, whom Saint-Exupéry portrays as a despairing, guilt-ridden man, communicates with the prince through radio in much the same way his wife keeps him informed of their child. The prince expresses his disappointment when his Earth Rose is replaced by a business-girt sage and the narrator comments that the prince was wrong about people. The prince meets a guardian of youth on his visit to Earth. </documents>
