[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "Four Weddings and a Funeral") Four Weddings and a Funeral Four Weddings and a Funeral is a 1994 British romantic comedy film directed by Mike Newell. It was the first of several films by screenwriter Richard Curtis to feature Hugh Grant, and follows the adventures of Charles (Grant) and his circle of friends through a number of social occasions as they each encounter romance. Andie MacDowell stars as Charles' love interest Carrie, with Kristin Scott Thomas, James Fleet, and Hugh Grant playing the lead characters along with a supporting cast. Claude Rains plays the role of Charles' father in the film. The story follows Charles' first unsuccessful relationship with Emily in college. Charles and Emily become engaged after the first summer of their engagement; however, during the second wedding, Emily dies of a sudden heart attack. Years later, the film cuts to Charles' second relationship with the soon-to-be ex-wife of Tom - Pauline. They are engaged in college, but Tom confesses that Charles' best friend Frank is in love with him. Charles declines the marriage proposal; he spends the next two years drinking heavily. Eventually, Charles moves on and becomes engaged again to Carrie. This marriage is also short-lived when Carrie dies during a flight from Paris - she suffocates to death with a plastic bag in her mouth. The third wedding, the most recent - the wedding of Charles and Tom - is the centerpiece of the film. At the wedding, Tom's son Gareth dies of a heart attack. The wedding is a disaster, and Tom offers to let Charles marry his other son, the actor Matthew. Later, the film cuts to Charles's journey to his younger brother Tom's funeral, where he meets Matthew. At Gareth's funeral, Matthew recites a poem as he reads the poem 'Funeral Blues' by W. H. Auden.</documents>
