[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: Coagulation) Coagulation Coagulation (also known as clotting) is the process by which blood changes from a liquid to a gel, forming a blood clot. It potentially results in hemostasis, the cessation of blood loss from a damaged vessel, followed by repair. The mechanism of coagulation involves activation, adhesion, and aggregation of platelets along with deposition and maturation of fibrin. Disorders of coagulation are disease conditions involving the excessive or insufficient formation of clots. Coagulation factor XIII (FXIII) plays a key role in stabilizing the fibrin clot and is the only coagulation factor present in the clot itself. </documents>
