[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "Ribosomal RNA") are found in all known forms of life. The ribosomal RNAs complex with proteins to form two subunits, the large subunit (LSU) and small subunit (SSU). During translation, mRNA is sandwiched between the small and large subunits, and the ribosome catalyzes the formation of a peptide bond between the two amino acids that are contained in the rRNA. A ribosome also has three binding sites called A, P, and E. In translation, messenger RNA is processed and then transported into the ribosome, where it becomes bound to a ribosome. Each ribosome recognizes a specific mRNA codon and translates it into the corresponding amino acid sequence. This is the process of translation.
[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 3(Title: "Multiple Ribosomes") Protein Synthesis The ribosome links amino acids together in the order specified by messenger RNA (mRNA) molecules. The large ribosomal subunit contains around 30S ribosomal subunits and the small contains 50S. Protein Synthesis Ribosomes link amino acids together in the order specified by messenger RNA (mRNA) molecules. The ribosomes and associated molecules are also known as the ""translational apparatus"". Ribosomes consist of two major components: the small ribosomal subunits, which read the RNA, and the large subunits, which join amino acids to form a polypeptide chain. Each subunit comprises one or more ribosomal RNA (rRNA) molecules and a variety of ribosomal proteins (r-protein or rProtein). Ribosomes attach to messenger RNA (mRNA) and when multiple ribosomes are bound to the same mRNA, they begin protein synthesis at different sites on the mRNA. This phenomenon is called polyribosome.</documents>
