[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "Botany Bay") An artefact collected on Cook's first voyage in Botany Bay is the bark shield left behind by a member of a local Aboriginal tribe. This very rare object is now in the British Museum's collection and was the subject of a programme in the BBC radio series A History of the World in 100 Objects. Lieutenant James Cook first landed at Kurnell, on the southern banks of Botany Bay, on Sunday 29 April 1770, when navigating his way up the New South Wales coast in search of a suitable site for a settlement. The HMS Endeavour later went on to anchor off the eastern head of the Bay, near the water inlet on the east side. This inlet is the present day Endeavour Bay. ... Archaeological research has uncovered the remains of the first European structure built at Botany Bay. The foundation of a stone enclosure was found and believed to have been constructed by settlers at the time of Lieutenant James Cook's landing in April 1770 on Kurnell Peninsula on southern shores of Botany Bay. </documents>
