[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m <documents>Doc 1(Title: "Preservation Society of Newport County") they changed their name to the Preservation Society of Newport County. Gertrude Conaway Vanderbilt, Harold Stirling Vanderbilt's widow, bequeathed $1.25 million to the society upon her death in 1978. Preservation Society of Newport County The Preservation Society of Newport County is a private, non-profit organization based in Newport, Rhode Island. It is Rhode Island's largest and most-visited cultural organization. The organization protects the architectural heritage of Newport County, especially the Bellevue Avenue Historic District. Seven of its 14 historic properties and landscapes are National Historic Landmarks, and most are open to the public. The Preservation Society of Newport County was
[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "Marble House") Marble House Marble House is a Gilded Age mansion in Newport, Rhode Island. Designed as a summer cottage for Alva and William Kissam Vanderbilt by the society architect Richard Morris Hunt, it was unparalleled in opulence for an American house when it was completed in 1892. Its temple-front portico, which also serves as a porte-cochère, resembles that of the White House. Located at 596 Bellevue Avenue, the house is now owned and operated by the Preservation Society of Newport County. Originally owned by William K Vanderbilt and made famous by his wife Alva Vanderbilt and the Society.</documents>
