[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m <documents>Doc 1(Title: "Córdoba, Spain") Cercadilla. From 784- 786 AD, Abd al-Rahman I built the Mezquita, or Great Mosque, of Córdoba, in the Umayyad style of architecture with variations inspired by indigenous Roman and Christian Visigothic structures. Later caliphs extended the mosque with more domed bays, arches, intricate mosaics and a minaret, making it one of the four wonders of the medieval Islamic world. After the Christian reconquest of Andalucía, a cathedral was built in the heart of the mosque, however much of the original structure remains. It can be found in the Historic Centre of Córdoba, a recognized World Heritage Site. Built in 930
[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba") a hall for teaching and for Sharia law cases during the rule of Abd al-Rahman and his successors. The Great Mosque of Córdoba exhibited features and an architectural appearance similar to the Great Mosque of Damascus, which may have been used as a model. The building is most notable for its arcaded hypostyle hall, with 80 Corinthian order columns of prostyle temples brought from Roman buildings. Spaniards and Andalusian Muslims, "reredos" decorate the interior walls, which are thick and richly decorated with mosaics and arcaded arcades. </documents>
