[36m(main_task pid=1120327)[0m Doc 2(Title: "Atahualpa Yupanqui") Atahualpa Yupanqui Atahualpa Yupanqui (; born Héctor Roberto Chavero; 31 January 1908 – 23 May 1992) was an Argentine singer, songwriter, guitarist, and writer. He is considered the most important Argentine folk musician of the 20th century. Yupanqui was born Héctor Roberto Chavero Aramburu in Pergamino, Buenos Aires Province. As a young man, he was taken in by a shoemaker. He studied woodworking and carpentry at a vocational school. He was influenced by folkloric folk musicians like Sixto Palavecino, Jorge Cafrune, Facundo Cabral, and later by Eduardo Falú, with whom he was close. He received musical training from piano teacher Luis Gianneo (1882–1966).</documents>
